------------------\nGeneric Reed-Solomon Codes Achieve List-Decoding Capacity — Lacuna (https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/generic-reed-solomon-codes-achieve-list-decoding-capacity)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; The authors prove that \"generic\" Reed-Solomon codes—those where evaluation points are chosen randomly from a sufficiently large field—can indeed reach the theoretical limit of list-decoding, resolving a major conjecture by Shangguan and Tamo. ...   * Resolution of Conjecture: The paper fully resolves Conjecture 5.7 from Shangguan and Tamo [ST20], which predicted the non-singularity of certain symbolic matrices associated with RS codes.\n  * Independence of Characteristic: The results hold regardless of the field's characteristic (whether it's a field of size $2^n$ or a prime field), which was not previously obvious.\n  * Algorithmic Efficiency: Beyond the existence proof, the authors provide a polynomial-time algorithm to compute the \"generic intersection dimension,\" a task that previously seemed to require checking an exponential number of partitions.\n\n## Key Results\n\n  * Theorem 1.5: Proves that a generic $[n, k]$-RS code with rate $R$ is $(\\rho, L)$-list decodable for a radius $\\rho = 1 - R - \\frac{1-R}{L+1}$. As the list size $L$ grows, this radius approaches the capacity $1-R$.\n  * Resolution of Conjecture: The paper fully resolves Conjecture 5.7 from Shangguan and Tamo [ST20], which predicted the non-singularity of certain symbolic matrices associated with RS codes.\n  * Gabidulin Codes: The authors extend their findings to show that generic Gabidulin codes (used in network coding) also achieve list-decoding capacity in the Hamming metric.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeneric Reed-Solomon Codes Achieve List-decoding Capacity (https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.05256)\nciteturn1academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.4 years ago; We also show that generic Reed-Solomon codes are $\\operatorname{MDS}(\\ell)$ for all $\\ell$, relying crucially on the GM-MDS theorem which shows that generator matrices of generic Reed-Solomon codes achieve any possible zero pattern. ... More concretely, we show that, with high probability, a random Reed-Solomon code of rate $R$ over an exponentially large field is list decodable from radius $1-R-\\epsilon$ with list size at most $\\frac{1-R-\\epsilon}{\\epsilon}$, resolving a conjecture of Shangguan and Tamo.\nTitle: Generic Reed-Solomon Codes Achieve List-decoding Capacity\nAuthors: Joshua Brakensiek, Sivakanth Gopi, Visu Makam\nDate: Fri Jun 10 17:54:02 2022\n\nIn a recent paper, Brakensiek, Gopi and Makam introduced higher order MDS codes as a generalization of MDS codes. An order-$\\ell$ MDS code, denoted by $\\operatorname{MDS}(\\ell)$, has the property that any $\\ell$ subspaces formed from columns of its generator matrix intersect as minimally as possible. An independent work by Roth defined a different notion of higher order MDS codes as those achieving a generalized singleton bound for list-decoding. In this work, we show that these two notions of higher order MDS codes are (nearly) equivalent. We also show that generic Reed-Solomon codes are $\\operatorname{MDS}(\\ell)$ for all $\\ell$, relying crucially on the GM-MDS theorem which shows that generator matrices of generic Reed-Solomon codes achieve any possible zero pattern. As a corollary, this implies that generic Reed-Solomon codes achieve list decoding capacity. More concretely, we show that, with high probability, a random Reed-Solomon code of rate $R$ over an exponentially large field is list decodable from radius $1-R-\\epsilon$ with list size at most $\\frac{1-R-\\epsilon}{\\epsilon}$, resolving a conjecture of Shangguan and Tamo.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Randomly Punctured Reed-Solomon Codes Achieve the List Decoding Capacity over Polynomia